@page "/components/icons"
@using Blaizio.Docs.Examples.Icons
<PageTitle>Icons - Blaizio</PageTitle>

<div class="space-y-10">
    <header class="docs-page-header space-y-2.5">
        <h1 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">Icons</h1>
        <p class="text-muted-foreground">The full Tabler icon set, rendered with <code>BzIcon</code>. Each member of <code>Icons.Outline</code> / <code>Icons.Filled</code> is a typed <code>Icon</code> value that knows its own family, so a single value is all you pass. Colour follows <code>currentColor</code>; size it with a CSS class.</p>
    </header>

    <UsageBlock Example="typeof(IconsUsageExample)"
                Description="Pass a typed Icon from Icons.Outline or Icons.Filled - no string, no family flag. Colour follows currentColor; size it with a class." />

    <ExamplesHeading />

    <Demo Title="Basic" Description="Pass an Icon from Icons.Outline or Icons.Filled - no string, no family flag."
          Example="typeof(IconsBasicExample)" />

    <Demo Title="Outline and filled" Description="The two Tabler families; the Icon carries which it is."
          Example="typeof(IconsFamiliesExample)" />

    <Demo Title="Sizing" Description="Set the size with a class (e.g. Tailwind size-*); the stroke scales with it."
          Example="typeof(IconsSizingExample)" />

    <Demo Title="Customization" Description="Colour follows currentColor, so tint an icon with any text-* utility - or drop it into a coloured chip. Size and colour are pure classes."
          Example="typeof(IconsCustomExample)" />

    <Demo Title="With tooltip" Description="Wrap an icon in a Tooltip for an accessible hint - the icon (in a span, so it can receive focus and hover) is the trigger via RenderAs."
          Example="typeof(IconsTooltipExample)" />

    <Demo Title="RTL" Description="Wrapped in a DirectionProvider - the icon row mirrors and flows from the right."
          Example="typeof(IconsRtlExample)" />

    <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20 text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight">Bundle size: you only ship what you use</h2>
        <p class="text-muted-foreground text-sm">The package carries the whole Tabler set (~6,000 icons), but a real app references a handful - and only those are shipped.</p>
        <BzAlert>
            <BzIcon Icon="@Icons.Outline.InfoCircle" />
            <BzAlertTitle>Keep trimming on for WebAssembly</BzAlertTitle>
            <BzAlertDescription>
                <code>Blaizio.Icons</code> is marked trimmable, and each icon is a self-contained expression-bodied
                property (no whole-set static constructor). So when you publish a Blazor WebAssembly app with IL
                trimming - the default for a <code>Release</code> publish (<code>PublishTrimmed</code>) - the trimmer
                drops every icon you never reference. In this docs app that takes <code>Blaizio.Icons</code> from
                <strong>~3.2 MB to ~5 KB</strong> over the wire. Leave <code>PublishTrimmed</code> enabled (it is by
                default); the only thing that defeats it is reaching the icons by reflection instead of a direct
                <code>Icons.Outline.X</code> reference. To check your <em>real</em> payload, open
                <code>publish/wwwroot/_framework</code> after a Release publish (the trimmed assembly there has a
                <code>.br</code> and no <code>.pdb</code>) - not the full ~3.2 MB copy in
                <code>bin/&hellip;/wwwroot/_framework</code> that <code>build</code> and <code>dotnet run</code> serve.
                On <strong>Blazor Server</strong> there is nothing to download - the assembly is loaded host-side - so
                its size is a non-issue there.
            </BzAlertDescription>
        </BzAlert>
    </section>
</div>

